master
22:29[pjb@thalassa org.xquartz:0 bocl 18Gi]$ 

======================================================================
DIAGNOSIS & RESOLUTION (v1.4.32)
--------------------------------
The module SYSTEM is not broken.  Verified end-to-end in a worktree:

- REQUIRE / PROVIDE and the boot-time module scan work.  At boot the prelude
  scans <prelude-dir>/modules/*/module.manifest into BOCL:*MODULE-LIST*; REQUIRE
  looks a module up there by its manifest :NAME (case-insensitive) and LOADs its
  files.  A dummy module dropped in <prelude-dir>/modules/ is found and loaded.
- With the ASDF module actually built and present, (require :asdf) SUCCEEDS:
  asdf.lisp loads and "ASDF" is pushed on *MODULES*.  It is just SLOW in the
  interpreter (~4-5 min to load the 14k-line single-file asdf.lisp).

Why the user hit UNDEFINED-MODULE-ERROR: the ASDF module was not built/installed
on that machine, so no modules/asdf/module.manifest existed to scan.  ASDF is
built from the modules/asdf-src git submodule (gitlink + .gitmodules ARE committed
at master, pinned to 91dacfd) via `make modules`, then `make install` copies
modules/asdf/ next to the prelude.  A plain `make build && make install` that
skips the submodule init silently produces NO asdf module (the modules/Makefile
asdf target no-ops when asdf-src/ is absent) — and the failure was then a
contentless "#<UNDEFINED-MODULE-ERROR>" with no message, a dead end.

Fix shipped:
- bocl::undefined-module-error now has a :REPORT (it printed as a bare
  #<UNDEFINED-MODULE-ERROR> before).  REQUIRE of a missing module now prints the
  module name, the modules directory that was searched (new BOCL:*MODULES-
  DIRECTORY*, set by the boot scan), the modules that WERE found there, and —
  for ASDF specifically — the exact commands to build and install it.

To actually get (require :asdf) working on an install:
    git submodule update --init modules/asdf-src
    make modules && make install     # needs a host CL (sbcl/ccl/clisp) to build
                                      # ASDF's single-file release
Then `(require :asdf)` loads it (slowly).  See modules/README.org.

Still rough (tracked separately, NOT blockers for this issue):
- Interpreter speed: loading asdf.lisp takes minutes; apropos over the image is
  ~20 s (see slow-apropos.issue).
- Making ASDF turnkey without the manual submodule step is a packaging decision
  (committing the 700 KB generated asdf.lisp vs. the current build-from-submodule
  design); left as-is per modules/README.org.
